'''Sant Bart''' ({{lang|ca|Sant Bart}}), officially '''Sant Bartomeu''', is the capital of [[Jala Province]], [[Balboa]], and the principal city of the [[Sant Bart (comarca)|Sant Bart comarca]]. With a population of approximately 2.82 million, it is the [[List of largest cities in Balboa|second-largest city in Balboa]] after [[Sant Cristòfor]]. The city is situated in the central highland valley of the [[Yalabirga Mountain Range]], at an elevation of approximately 1,200 metres, and is the political, commercial, and cultural centre of the Jala highlands.

Sant Bart served as the national capital of the [[Republic of Balboa (1899–1973)|rump state]] — colloquially known as "Balboa Boba" — from 1899 to 1973, following the loss of [[Sant Cristòfor]] and the [[Balboa Canal]] to [[Zong]] in the [[Second War of the Isthmus]]. The city's transformation from a small highland village to a major metropolitan centre was driven by this period, during which it became the seat of the truncated republic's government, industry, and administration. Following reunification in 1973, Sant Bart lost its status as national capital but remained the principal administrative centre of Jala Province.

== Geography ==

[[File:Sant Bart Yalabirga slopes placeholder.jpg|thumb|280px|The Yalabirga cloud forest slopes rising above the western edge of Sant Bart]]
<!-- Image prompt: The western edge of a highland city where urban development meets steep cloud-forested mountain slopes. Dense tropical cloud forest covers the mountainside above the city, with mist clinging to the upper slopes. Below, residential streets with mid-rise apartment buildings transition into the rugged terrain. The contrast between the built environment and the natural cloud forest is stark. Cool, misty highland atmosphere with soft light filtering through clouds. -->

Sant Bart occupies a highland valley in the central portion of the [[Yalabirga Mountain Range]], approximately 120 kilometres east of the [[Aethiopian Ocean]] coast. The city's territory spans 2,761 km² and extends from the Yalabirga ridgeline in the west to the foothills and coastal plain approaches in the east. The [[Riu Noguera]] flows through the city from the northwest, draining the central highlands eastward toward the Aethiopian plain.

The western edge of the city rises steeply along the Yalabirga slopes, with elevations exceeding 1,800 metres in the cloud forest zone. The central valley, where the bulk of the urban area is concentrated, sits at approximately 1,200 metres. The eastern portion of the city descends gradually toward the Aethiopian coastal plain, with progressively lower elevations and gentler terrain toward the far eastern zones.

The city's highland location gives it a significantly cooler and less humid climate than the coastal cities of [[Sant Cristòfor]] and [[Costabella]], which has historically made it a preferred residential location for the Jala planter elite and, later, for the professional and political classes.

=== Municipal boundaries ===

The municipality of Sant Bart has an unusual, elongated shape that reflects a complex history of suburban secession and territorial strategy. The city's core occupies the central highland valley, but a narrow corridor extends eastward along the highway for over 100 kilometres to reach the [[Aethiopian Ocean]] coast — a feature visible in the long, narrow form of [[Zona 12 (Sant Bart)|Zona 12]].

The municipality was originally much larger and more compact, encompassing the western suburbs and the highland slopes. Over the course of the late nineteenth and twentieth centuries, however, the western suburbs — including present-day [[La Creueta]] and other municipalities — progressively seceded from the municipality of Sant Bart, establishing themselves as independent municipalities within the [[Sant Bart (comarca)|Sant Bart comarca]]. The western suburbs, dominated by the planter elite and later by the professional class, sought greater local control over land use, taxation, and services, and chafed under the administration of a city government increasingly dominated by working-class and immigrant political interests.

As the western suburbs departed, the city government fought to retain access to the Aethiopian coast, which was vital for trade, logistics, and industrial development. The result was the preservation of the eastern highway corridor — a narrow strip of municipal territory following the main east–west highway from the city centre to the coast. This corridor, now largely occupied by warehouses, logistics facilities, and coastal suburbs, ensures that Sant Bart remains a coastal municipality despite having lost much of its western territory. The city's unusual shape — compact in the centre, with a long eastern tail — is a direct product of this history.

