Sant Bart has a {{w|subtropical highland climate}} (Köppen ''Cwb''), with mild temperatures year-round and a pronounced wet–dry seasonal cycle. Average daily highs range from 22 to 25 °C and lows from 10 to 12 °C, with little seasonal variation. The wet season runs from May to October, with the heaviest rainfall occurring from June to August; the dry season from November to April is characterised by clear skies, low humidity, and cool nights. Annual rainfall averages approximately 1,420 mm in the city centre.

The city's position in a highland valley, with the Yalabirga range rising to the west and highland ridges to the south, creates an unusual microclimate. Moisture-laden trade winds from the [[Aethiopian Ocean]] to the east flow across the valley and are forced upward by the Yalabirga slopes to the west, producing enhanced {{w|orographic lift}} and frequent afternoon thunderstorms over the western zones during the wet season. The Yalabirga cloud forest zone above 1,600 metres receives up to 2,500 mm annually. The southern highlands block some weather systems from the south, while the western mountains shelter the city from the harshest weather approaching from the Shendan side. The surrounding mountains also reduce wind speeds in the valley, creating calm conditions for much of the year.

The valley topography produces strong {{w|temperature inversion}}s during the dry season, when cold air drains down from the surrounding mountains into the valley floor at night. Nighttime temperatures can occasionally drop below 8 °C during the driest months (December–February), producing unusually cool conditions for the latitude, while daytime highs remain mild and pleasant.

