=== Central zones ===

[[File:Sant Bart CBD placeholder.jpg|thumb|280px|The central business district (Zona 2), with the Yalabirga range visible to the west]]
<!-- Image prompt: The central business district of a highland capital city. Modern office towers of 15–25 storeys rise from a compact grid of boulevards, with a mix of glass curtain-wall buildings and older stone-faced structures from the mid-20th century. Street-level retail and pedestrian activity. Mountains visible in the background above the rooftops. Clear, bright highland light with deep blue sky. -->

'''Zona 1''' is the city's government and cultural district. It contains the [[Comarca Government HQ]], [[Sant Bart Main Station|Main Station]], the [[Great Library of Sant Bart|Great Library]], the [[Sant Bart Opera House]], and the [[National History Museum of Balboa|National History Museum]], making it the city's principal administrative, entertainment, and cultural centre. The zone is densely built up with high-rise apartment buildings along the [[Metro de Sant Bart|Metro]] corridors. The majority of the city's population lives in Zona 1 and the adjacent Zona 2, which together form the compact urban core of Sant Bart.

'''Zona 2''' is the city's central business district, the commercial and financial core of Sant Bart. The zone is the smallest by area but one of the densest, with office towers, banks, corporate headquarters, and retail concentrated in a compact core. The [[Sant Bart North Station|northern railway station]] and the [[Estadi Nacional (Sant Bart)|Estadi Nacional]] — the largest stadium in Balboa — are located here. The CBD developed rapidly during the rump state period, when Sant Bart became the national capital, and was further expanded during the post-reunification era.

'''Zona 4''', east of the CBD, is the wealthiest zone in the city. It is characterised by mansions and high-end residential properties, with some middle-class housing in the southern portion. The [[Presidential Palace (Sant Bart)|Presidential Palace]], the former residence of the President of Balboa during the rump state period, is located here. Following reunification, the palace was converted to the official residence of the [[Governor of Jala Province]].

[[File:Sant Bart Presidential Palace placeholder.jpg|thumb|280px|The Presidential Palace in Zona 4, now the residence of the Governor of Jala Province]]
<!-- Image prompt: A grand early-20th-century presidential palace in a highland city. A neoclassical building with a symmetrical facade, columns, and a pedimented entrance, set in formal gardens with palm trees and highland vegetation. The building is painted white with stone detailing. Cool, clear highland light with mountains visible behind the building. -->

=== Northern and western zones ===

'''Zona 3''', to the northwest, is a suburban zone with upper-class homes and mid-rise buildings. It is largely residential, with a lower density than the central zones.

'''Zona 5''', in the far north, is a mostly suburban zone with detached housing and low-density development. It extends toward the highland ridges and is the northernmost zone in the city.

'''Zona 6''', northwest of the city centre, is a suburban zone in the highland foothills. It is largely residential, with a mix of detached housing and low-rise development, and serves as a transition area between the suburban north and the rural highlands to the west.

'''Zona 7''', the largest zone by area, covers the Yalabirga mountain slope to the west and a large expanse of highland terrain. The zone is mostly rural and mountainous, with a density of only 85 inhabitants per km². The upper slopes contain communities of the [[Birú people]], the indigenous inhabitants of the Jala highlands, who have maintained a presence in the remote highlands throughout the colonial and post-colonial periods.

=== Southern zones ===

'''Zona 8''', in the south, is a mixed residential and industrial zone. The northern neighbourhoods are residential, ranging from middle class to working class, while the south is dominated by industrial facilities and warehouses. [[Cecilio Ríos International Airport]] is located in the southern portion of the zone.

'''Zona 9''', in the southwest, has a similar pattern: middle-class residential in the north and working-class housing with extensive warehouses and industrial facilities in the south.

'''Zona 10''', south of the CBD, is a densely populated working-class zone with mid-rise apartment buildings and some light industry.

=== Far eastern zones ===

'''Zona 11''', on the eastern edge of the city, is a small zone with a suburban character mixed with warehouses and light industry. It serves as a transition area between the urban core and the outer municipalities.

'''Zona 12''', the easternmost zone, extends to the [[Aethiopian Ocean]] coast. The western portion is a narrow strip along the highway with warehouses and logistics facilities, while the eastern portion contains coastal suburbs. The zone is the most easterly in the city and the closest to the Aethiopian coast.

== History ==

{{main|History of Balboa}}

=== Pre-colonial and colonial period ===

The Sant Bart valley was originally inhabited by the [[Birú people]], who maintained settlements in the highland valleys and footpaths across the Yalabirga. The Birú highland confederation controlled the portage corridors connecting the Aethiopian and Shendan coasts, and the Sant Bart valley was a minor waystation on the trans-isthmian route.

The Volisanian colonial administration established plantations in the surrounding highland valleys, where the cooler climate and fertile volcanic soils were suitable for coffee and sugar cultivation. Sant Bart itself, however, remained a small village throughout the colonial period. The Aethiopian coast to the east was rough, hilly terrain with little settlement or development. The Birú population was devastated by conquest, disease, and forced labour, and the survivors retreated into the remote highlands.

=== Independence and the Velásquez era (1854–1893) ===

Following independence in 1854, the Jala planters dominated the post-independence political system, and the [[Redilution]] and [[Birthing movement|Birthing]] programmes encouraged Illypnian immigration to the highland valleys. The [[Sant Bart's Coup]] of 1888 and the Velásquez government's industrialisation programme established the first manufacturing enterprises in the Sant Bart corridor, but the village remained small and the surrounding area largely undeveloped.

=== Rump state capital (1899–1973) ===

[[File:Sant Bart rump state era placeholder.jpg|thumb|280px|Sant Bart in the 1930s, during the rump state period, showing the rapid expansion of the former highland village into a capital city]]
<!-- Image prompt: A highland city in the 1930s, showing rapid expansion from a small village to a national capital. Low-rise colonial-era buildings alongside newer government offices and apartment blocks under construction. Dirt roads transitioning to paved boulevards. The city fills a highland valley with mountains visible behind. Sepia-toned or vintage photographic style, showing the mix of old and new architecture characteristic of a hastily-built capital. -->

The [[Second War of the Isthmus]] (1893–1899) transformed Sant Bart's trajectory. With the loss of Sant Cristòfor and the Balboa Canal to [[Zong]], the capital of the rump state — the "[[Republic of Balboa (1899–1973)|Balboa Boba]]" — was relocated to Sant Bart. Almost overnight, the small highland village became the political and administrative centre of the truncated republic. Internal migrants from the agricultural districts flocked to the new capital seeking employment, and the city grew rapidly. The rough hills of the Aethiopian slope began to see settlement and development for the first time, and the surrounding municipalities developed along the rail corridors as industrial satellites and commuter settlements.

The planter elite maintained its political and economic dominance throughout the rump state period. The Presidential Palace was constructed in the eastern district (present-day Zona 4) to house the presidency, and government offices were established in the central district (present-day Zona 1). The city's industrial base expanded with textiles, processed foodstuffs, and basic metal goods, and the population grew steadily through internal migration from the agricultural districts.

The [[Great Adonian War]] (1935–1947) disrupted the city's development. Sant Bart was occupied by Zong forces during the Balboa Campaign, and the city's population was subjected to occupation conditions for the duration of the war. The [[Estadi Nacional (Sant Bart)|Estadi Nacional]], the largest stadium on the isthmus, was requisitioned by the Zong military for use as a staging area.

=== Post-reunification era (1973–present) ===

The reunification of 1973, following the [[Treaty of Areza]], reintegrated the Sant Cristòfor and canal territories into the Balboan state. Sant Bart lost its status as national capital but remained the principal administrative centre of Jala Province. The Presidential Palace was converted to the official residence of the Governor of Jala Province, and many national institutions were relocated to Sant Cristòfor.

The post-reunification period saw significant expansion of the Sant Bart metropolitan area. The [[Metro de Sant Bart]] was constructed, providing the city with a three-line rapid transit system. The suburban rail network was developed, connecting the city to the surrounding municipalities. The southern and eastern zones experienced significant industrial and residential growth, and the city's population continued to expand through internal migration from the rural highlands.

== Demographics ==

{{main|Demographics of Balboa}}

Sant Bart's population of approximately 2.82 million (2026) makes it the [[List of largest cities in Balboa|second-largest city in Balboa]]. The city's demographic composition reflects the history of the Jala highlands: the [[Redilution]] and [[Birthing movement|Birthing]] programmes, which encouraged large-scale Illypnian immigration to the highland valleys, resulted in a significantly higher proportion of [[White Balboan people|White Balboans]] than the national average.

