Line 1 is the main south-to-north line through the urban core, connecting the government and cultural district in Zona 1 with the CBD and Zona 2. Line 2 runs parallel to Line 1, extending northward beyond the city limits to serve the suburban municipalities. Line 3 runs diagonally from southwest to northeast, connecting the wealthy residential Zona 4 through the city centre to the northeastern reaches of Zona 4.

=== Rail ===

[[File:Sant Bart Main Station placeholder.jpg|thumb|280px|Sant Bart Main Station, the principal railway hub of the Jala highlands]]
<!-- Image prompt: A grand railway station in a highland capital city. A large vaulted terminal building with a facade of stone and glass, combining early-20th-century architecture with modern renovations. Platforms visible with regional and long-distance trains. The station concourse is busy with passengers. Highland light streams through the glass roof. -->

[[Sant Bart Main Station]], located in Zona 1, is the city's principal railway station and the hub of the national rail network in the Jala highlands. All seven [[Rodalies de Sant Bart]] suburban lines and all seven long-distance services operate from Main Station. [[Sant Bart North Station]], in Zona 2, is a secondary terminus handling long-distance services northward toward the highland comarcas and the Tramuntana range.

The [[Rodalies de Sant Bart]] suburban rail system operates seven lines (A through G) radiating from Sant Bart, serving the comarca's municipalities and connecting to neighbouring comarcas. Seven long-distance rail services also operate from Sant Bart, connecting the city to destinations across Balboa.

=== Air ===

[[Cecilio Ríos International Airport]], located in Zona 8 in the southern portion of the city, is the principal airport of the Jala highlands, offering domestic and international routes. The airport is the principal gateway to the highland region and handles both passenger and cargo traffic.

=== Roads ===

Sant Bart is connected to the national expressway network by eleven expressways converging on the city, linking it to the rest of Jala Province and the national network. The city's road network includes the central boulevards of the CBD, the ring roads encircling the urban core, and the highway corridors extending east to the Aethiopian coast and west to the Sant Bart Pass.

